# Terraform variable files. These are where a secret is most likely to be typed
# by hand — terraform.tfvars is the default filename Terraform auto-loads, and
# it is the natural place to paste a Graph client secret or a ROOTKey API key
# while testing. This repository is public, so a single careless `git add .`
# would publish them. Ignored everywhere, including in the connector subfolders.
#
# The .tfvars.example convention is explicitly allowed through, for documented
# templates that contain placeholders only.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!*.tfvars.example

# Terraform state. Also covered by each connector's own .gitignore; repeated
# here so a state file written anywhere in the tree is caught.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
.terraform/
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Saved plan files. A saved plan embeds the values of the root module's input
# variables, so it is as sensitive as a state file even though it looks like a
# build artifact.
*.tfplan
tfplan

# Internal working material that must never ship to a customer. The public
# documentation lives in the separate ROOT-Key/docs repository, so a docs/
# folder here is scratch by definition — today it holds BACKEND_CONTRACT_v2.md,
# ROOTKey's internal backend API contract. It is untracked, and on a public
# repository "untracked" is one absent-minded `git add .` away from published.
/docs/
